{{Short description|Logistics of World War II invasion}}
{{good article}}
{{Use US English|date=July 2025}}
{{Use DMY dates|date=July 2025}}
[[File:"M. D. Shore, S1-c, operating a forklift truck at the Navy supply depot at Guam, Marianas.", 06-08-1945 - NARA - 520685.jpg|thumb|right|upright=1.3|Operating a forklift truck at the Navy supply depot on Guam]]
{{Campaignbox Marianas and Palaus}}

[[Operation Forager]], the U.S. invasion of the [[Mariana Islands]] in June and July 1944 during [[World War II]], was the largest amphibious operation of the [[Pacific war]] to that date. It involved the movement and support of the [[Fifth Fleet]] and two [[corps]] of ground troops over extremely long distances. A crucial problem was the provision of sufficient shipping to maintain the fleet and ground force. In the event, a shortage of ships and cargo space forced units to leave some vehicles and unit equipment behind, and some units had to travel on separate ships than their unit equipment. Unloading was facilitated by the use of wooden [[pallet]]s, but not all cargo ships had the space or equipment to accommodate them, and on [[Battle of Guam (1944)|Guam]] the reef prevented landing craft directly accessing the beach and required stores to be transferred to [[DUKW]]s or [[Landing Vehicle Tracked|LVT]]s on the reef.

Two dozen [[replenishment oiler]]s were on hand to enable the fleet to refuel at sea. For the first time in the [[Central Pacific Area]], the Japanese targeted oilers. With the fleet at sea for over four months, many stores aboard ran low. These were replenished by [[general stores issue ship]]s carrying 5,000 different items, such as toilet paper. The [[Battle of the Philippine Sea]] and staunch Japanese resistance ashore on [[Battle of Saipan|Saipan]] and later Guam resulted in a longer campaign than anticipated, with increased calls for naval gunfire support, and consequently prodigious ammunition consumption. Most resupply ships were not [[Combat loading#vertically|vertically loaded]], which made retrieving the most urgently needed ammunition more problematic. [[Ammunition ship]]s were called forward to replenish the fleet off Saipan while the battle still raged on shore, a risky action, as Japanese air attacks occurred almost every day.

After the battle, the Mariana Islands were developed into a major naval and air base. Hospitals on the islands received wounded from the [[Battle of Iwo Jima]] and the [[Battle of Okinawa]]. [[Boeing B-29 Superfortress]] bombers were based on the islands and participated in the campaign of [[air raids on Japan]], including the [[bombing of Tokyo (10 March 1945)|bombing of Tokyo on 10 March 1945]] and the [[atomic bombings of Hiroshima and Nagasaki]].

==Background==
[[File:Initial supply by classes for Saipan, Sicily, Luzon and Normandy.jpg|thumb|right|Initial supply by classes for Saipan, [[Operation Husky|Sicily]], [[Battle of Luzon|Luzon]] and [[Operation Overlord|Normandy]] ]]
[[Operation Forager]], the U.S. invasion of the [[Mariana Islands]] during [[World War II]] involved the movement and support of a fleet of ships and two corps of ground troops over a distance of {{convert|3,200|mi}} from Hawaii.{{sfn|Crowl|1960|p=47}} It was the largest amphibious operation of the [[Pacific war]] to that date.{{sfn|Crowl|1960|p=20}} The American forces were part of Admiral [[Raymond A. Spruance]]'s [[Fifth Fleet]]. The Joint Amphibious Forces (Task Force 51) were led by [[Vice admiral (United States)|Vice Admiral]] [[Richmond K. Turner]] and the expeditionary troops (Task Force 56) by Lieutenant General [[Holland M. Smith]].{{sfn|Shaw|Nalty|Turnbladh|1994|p=239}}

The Mariana islands were mountainous, with a total land area of about {{convert|72|sqmi|ha}}. This meant there was little chance of the islands being overrun quickly unlike the small, low-lying coral atolls captured in the [[Gilbert and Marshall Islands campaign]], and prolonged fighting was a possibility. Accordingly, the assault troops carried 32 days' supply of rations, 30 days worth of medical supplies, 20 days' supply of fuel,{{sfn|Shaw|Nalty|Turnbladh|1994|pp=242–243}} and seven units of fire for all weapons, except anti-aircraft guns, for which ten units of fire were taken.{{sfn|Morison|1953|pp=346–347}} This represented {{convert|1.22|ST|t}} per soldier, considerably more than the {{convert|0.84|ST|t}} per soldier of the US forces engaged in [[Operation Husky]], the Allied invasion of Sicily the year before. This was because the supply lines to Sicily were reasonably secure, whereas for Operation Forager, intervention and disruption by Japanese air and naval forces was anticipated.{{sfn|Bernard|1948|p=52}}

The first objective in Operation Forager was the [[Battle of Saipan|occupation of Saipan]], which commenced on 15 June 1944,{{sfn|Morison|1953|p=186}} after a two-day naval bombardment,{{sfn|Morison|1953|pp=179–183}} around the same time the [[Allies in World War II|Allies]] were invading France in [[Operation Overlord]].{{sfn|Morison|1953|pp=161–162}} The [[2nd Marine Division]], [[4th Marine Division]], and the Army's [[27th Infantry Division (United States)|27th Infantry Division]] engaged in costly fighting before the island was declared secure on 9 July.{{sfn|Crowl|1960|pp=264–265}} In addition to the fighting on land, there was a naval battle, the [[Battle of the Philippine Sea]], on 19-20 June.{{sfn|Crowl|1960|pp=122–123}} On 24 July 1944, the 4th Marine Division landed on nearby Tinian, supported by aerial and naval bombardment and the guns of the [[XXIV Corps (United States)|XXIV Corps Artillery]], firing across the strait from Saipan.{{sfn|Shaw|Nalty|Turnbladh|1994|pp=361–364}}{{sfn|Morison|1953|pp=359–360}} Instead of landing in the southwest, the Marines landed on the northwest coast, where there were two very small beaches that were lightly defended.{{sfn|Crowl|1960|pp=271–274}} The island was declared secure on 1 August, but mopping up operations continued until 1 January 1945.{{sfn|Crowl|1960|p=303}} The [[Battle of Guam (1944)|assault on Guam]] was originally scheduled for 18 June, just three days after the landing on Saipan, but the Battle of the Philippine Sea and stubborn resistance by the unexpectedly large Japanese garrison on Saipan led to the invasion of Guam being postponed for over a month to allow the Army's [[77th Infantry Division (United States)|77th Infantry Division]] to be brought in from Hawaii.{{sfn|Morison|1953|p=371}} The American forces landed on Guam on 21 July,{{sfn|Crowl|1960|pp=338–339}} and on 10 August organized Japanese resistance declared ended,{{sfn|Shaw|Nalty|Turnbladh|1994|pp=566–567}} but as many as 10,000 Japanese soldiers were still be at large.{{sfn|Shaw|Nalty|Turnbladh|1994|p=568}}

==Naval logistics==

===Shipping===
The provision of shipping to maintain the force was a critical problem. A staff study by [[Captain (United States Navy)|Captain]] John F. Rees, the chief of the transportation section on the staff of the Commander in Chief of the [[United States Pacific Fleet|Pacific Fleet]], Admiral [[Chester W. Nimitz]], estimated this would require 120 cargo ships and 58 troop transports, of which only 18 and 22 respectively were on hand in April 1944.{{sfn|Morison|1953|pp=347–349}}{{sfn|Ancell|Miller|1996|pp=588, 599}} For the [[invasion of Saipan]], 110 transport vessels were available: 37 [[attack transport]]s (APA) and [[troopship|troop transport]]s (AP); 11 [[attack cargo ship]]s (AKA) and [[cargo ship]]s (AK); 5 [[dock landing ship]]s (LSD); 47 [[Landing Ship, Tank|tank landing ships]] (LST); and 10 [[high-speed transport]]s (APD). They carried {{convert|74,986.6|MTON}} of cargo. Staging areas were widely separated; the 2nd Marine Division loaded at [[Hilo, Hawaii|Hilo]] on the [[island of Hawaii]]; the 4th Marine Division at [[Kahului, Hawaii|Kahului]] on [[Maui]], and the 27th Infantry Division on [[Oahu]].{{sfn|Crowl|1960|pp=47–48}} All the [[combatant ship]]s involved in the operation were American, as were all but three of the [[auxiliary ship]]s, two Dutch merchant vessels and a Norwegian [[factory ship#whaler factory|whaler factory ship]] converted to use as an [[oil tanker]].{{sfn|Morison|1953|p=162}}

[[Combat loading]], in which the most urgently required cargo was stowed in ships' holds so as to be immediately accessible, was favoured for ships carrying assault troops, but was not always possible due to the shortage of shipping. Some units of the [[V Amphibious Corps]] and the XXIV Corps Artillery found themselves travelling separately from their unit equipment, a highly undesirable scenario in a combat zone, but fully combat loading the entire force would have meant leaving up to 35 percent of their cargo behind.{{sfn|Crowl|1960|pp=47–48}} Even then, there was insufficient cargo space, and the V Amphibious Corps was forced to make cuts to unit equipment. This fell most heavily on transport, which was bulky. Only 94 percent of ambulances, 83 percent of trucks, 71 percent of trailers and 75 percent of tractors could be taken to Saipan.{{sfn|Crowl|1960|pp=128–129}}

[[File:Caterpillar tractor pulling ammunition pallets from an LCM.jpg|thumb|left|Unloading ammunition on pallets from an LCM]]
This was partially offset by the allocation of 185 [[DUKW]]s. Each division had a DUKW company: the 1st Amphibian Truck Company for the 2nd Marine Division, the 2nd Amphibian Truck Company for the 4th Marine Division, and a provisional amphibian truck company for the 27th Infantry Division. In addition, the 477th Amphibian Truck Company was attached to the XXIV Corps Artillery.{{sfn|Crowl|1960|pp=128–129}} For Guam, the [[3rd Marine Division]] and the [[1st Provisional Marine Brigade]] shared the [[III Amphibious Corps]] Motor Transport Battalion, with its Company C assigned to the brigade.{{sfn|Lodge|1954|p=194}} When a typhoon struck on 29 July, an LST broached and LVTs and landing craft were unable to operate, but the DUKWs proved more seaworthy, and they were able to continue delivering supplies.{{sfn|Crowl|1960|pp=300–301}}  

The V Amphibious Corps Headquarters ordered 25 to 50 percent of all supplies and two to five units of fire of ammunition be loaded on [[pallet]]s. This order was embraced by the 27th Infantry Division, which put between 80 and 90 percent of its supplies on pallets, but the 2nd and 4th Marine Divisions were less enthusiastic about the idea. The former put no more than 25 percent of its supplies on pallets, and the latter only palletized 10 to 15 percent.{{sfn|Crowl|1960|pp=48–49}}{{sfn|Shaw|Nalty|Turnbladh|1994|pp=242–243}} The 3rd Marine Division and the 1st Provisional Marine Brigade did not put any of their supplies on pallets at all, but the 77th Infantry Division complied with the directive and constructed more than 5,000 {{convert|4|by|6|ft|adj=on}} wooden pallets, although about 1,000 pallet loads were broken up because they did not fit into the available cargo space or because a ship lacked the equipment to handle pallets.{{sfn|Bruce|1944|p=6}}{{sfn|Crowl|1960|pp=319–320}}

On Smith's recommendation, resupply ships sailing from the United States were "block loaded", with a mixture of common stores. This proved to not only be inefficient in the use of cargo space, but presented difficulty in unloading, as each type of supplies had to be completely unloaded before the next could be accessed. In his evaluation, Turner recommended the Navy revert to commodity loading, where each ship carried a particular type of supplies, which facilitated selective unloading of stores.{{sfn|Shaw|Nalty|Turnbladh|1994|pp=242–243}}

===Food and water===
The Southern Attack Force was mounted from the [[South Pacific Area]], so most of its initial complement of fresh provisions came from New Zealand, while those for the Northern Attack Force, destined for the assaults on [[Saipan]] and [[Tinian]], came from Hawaii and the [[West Coast of the United States]]. Once the operation was under way, resupply was from the West Coast though the bases in Hawaii, and on [[Majuro]], [[Kwajalein Atoll|Kwajalein]] and [[Eniwetok]].{{sfn|Morison|1953|pp=343–344}} Rations for Army units were drawn for Army stocks while those for the Navy and Marine Corps came from Navy stocks.{{sfn|Morison|1953|pp=343–344}} Resupply direct from the United States was necessary because the base facilities in Hawaii were insufficient to handle the volume of supplies required for mounting simultaneous operations against Saipan and Guam.{{sfn|Bernard|1948|pp=49–50}} Ships returning from the forward areas to supply points transferred excess stores not required for the return voyage to other vessels.{{sfn|Carter|1953|p=139}} 

[[File:USMC 87615 Marines load supplies aboard two LSTs in preparation for the assault on Tinian.jpg|thumb|right|Marines load supplies aboard two LSTs in preparation for the assault on Tinian]]
The supply of fresh produce depended on the availability of [[reefer ship]]s, cargo ships with refrigerated [[Hold (compartment)|holds]]. These were specially designed to carry a mixture of fresh and dry provisions, and to permit selective discharge of a balanced cargo so one ship after another could be revictualed without having to unload the reefer's cargo and re-stow it. The Service Force had nine reefers, as the majority of the Navy's reefers were serving with the [[United States Atlantic Fleet|Atlantic Fleet]]. The Pacific Fleet's reefers were therefore supplemented by ships of the [[War Shipping Administration]]. These were large {{convert|5,000|grt|adj=on|lk=on}} vessels with [[United States Merchant Marine|Merchant Marine]] crews that could carry up to {{convert|348,000|ft}} of refrigerated produce, enough for 30 days' supply for 90,000 soldiers; but their holds and [[Hold (compartment) #Cargo hatch|hatches]] were not organised for selective discharge, so they had to unload into [[warehouse]]s, and Saipan had no such facilities until 15 August. Nonetheless, during Operation Forager, the Service Force managed to supply fresh produce to all combatant ships on five days out of six, and to the forces ashore for one day in three.{{sfn|Morison|1953|pp=343–344}}

Until water supplies could be secured ashore, arrangements were made for transports, LSTs and large warships to provide water to smaller vessels lacking sufficient water storage. Water supplies ran low when these ships started to leave, and bad weather sometimes prevented resupply from those that remained.{{sfn|Carter|1953|p=162}} The 77th Infantry Division took with it {{convert|190,000|USgal|L}} of potable water in {{convert|5|USgal|adj=on}} [[jerrycan]]s and {{convert|55|USgal|adj=on}} drums.{{sfn|Bruce|1944|p=3}} Each shore party engineer battalion had two distillation units.{{sfn|Crowl|1960|p=318}} The division moved six of them to [[Pago Bay]] to keep forward units in northern Guam supplied, and managed to capture the well at the village of [[Barrigada, Guam|Barrigada]] intact, which could supply {{convert|30,000|USgal|L}} per day.{{sfn|Bruce|1944|p=3}} With the fleet at sea for over four months, many stores aboard ran low. These were replenished by [[general stores issue ship]]s carrying 5,000 different items such as toilet paper.{{sfn|Morison|1953|pp=343–344}}

===Fuel===
[[File:US Army Air Corps photo B-63655AC.jpg|thumb|left|USS ''LST-205'' beached on a reef along with two other LSTs during landing operations on Saipan, Marianas Islands, 21 June 1944, as men of the US Army Air Corps 804th Engineer Aviation Battalion roll floating gasoline drums from the ship to shore.]]
Fleet oilers were under the command of [[Commodore (United States)|Commodore]] Augustus (Gus) H. Gray, the commander of Service Squadron 8. Captain Burton B. Biggs, an experienced logistics officer on Spruance's staff, planned and then directed fueling operations.{{sfn|Morison|1953|p=345}} Biggs estimated Operation Forager would require {{convert|100,000|oilbbl}} per day, roughly the capacity of a single oiler.{{sfn|Wildenberg|1996|pp=183–185}} Eight task units were organized, designated Task Units 16.7.1 to 16.7.8, each of which had three oilers, escorted by at least two [[destroyer]]s or [[destroyer escort]]s.{{sfn|Carter|1953|p=140}}

The oilers were dispatched to designated refueling areas, {{convert|75|by|25|mi|adj=on}} rectangles of open ocean; eleven were designated for Operation Forager, each named after a well-known oil company.{{sfn|Carter|1953|p=139}} In accordance with a timetable, or occasionally when there was a special need for more fuel, ships would leave their task groups and rendezvous with the oilers to be refueled at sea.{{sfn|Morison|1953|p=345}} Oilers returned to Eniwetok when they had less than {{convert|20,000|oilbbl}},{{sfn|Carter|1953|p=139}} where they were refilled by commercial tankers.{{sfn|Carter|1953|p=140}}

On 18 June, the oilers {{USS|Saranac|AO-74|6}}, {{USS|Neshanic||2}} and {{USS|Saugatuck||2}} were refueling four destroyers and destroyer escorts when they came under attack by five Japanese bombers. This was the first time the Japanese had targeted oilers in the Central Pacific. All three oilers were hit. ''Saranac'' had eight killed and twenty-two wounded and was so badly damaged it had to return to Eniwetok and then to [[San Pedro, California]], for repairs. A bomb exploded among gasoline drums on ''Neshanic''{{'}}s deck, setting off a major fire that was extinguished by its damage control party. ''Neshanic'' and ''Saugatuck'' were repaired by Service Squadron 10 at Eniwetok.{{sfn|Morison|1953|p=345}}{{sfn|Wildenberg|1996|pp=183–185}}<ref>{{cite web |title=Saranac IV (AO-74)|publisher=Naval History and Heritage Command|url=https://www.history.navy.mil/content/history/nhhc/research/histories/ship-histories/danfs/s/saranac-iv.html|access-date=8 July 2025|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210924190625/https://www.history.navy.mil/content/history/nhhc/research/histories/ship-histories/danfs/s/saranac-iv.html|url-status=live|archive-date=24 September 2021}}</ref>

===Ammunition===
Ammunition usage is less predictable than food or fuel, but orders had to be placed with the depots months in advance. Fleet and aircraft ammunition was then brought forward by the [[ammunition ship]]s {{USS|Lassen|AE-3|6}}, {{USS|Rainier|AE-5|2}}, {{USS|Shasta|AE-6|2}}, {{USS|Mauna Loa|AE-8|2}}, {{USS|Mazama|AE‑9|2}} and {{USS|Sangay|AE-10|2}}, which replenished warships at Eniwetok lagoon from 15 June on.{{sfn|Carter|1953|p=139}}{{sfn|Morison|1953|pp=346–347}} Because the [[Pacific Ocean Area]] had only six ammunition ships, the LSD {{USS|Ashland|LSD-1|6}} was pressed into service to deliver ammunition, as were twenty-six LSTs. Six [[Victory ship]]s were chartered from the War Shipping Administration to haul ammunition, the first of which arrived off Saipan on 30 June. They were loaded on the West Coast with a mix of ammunition in such a way as to permit optional discharge, whereby any particular ammunition could be retrieved without having to remove layers of unwanted other types. This was the first time this had been tried with merchant ships, and it was risky because there was a shortage of crewmen trained in the proper handing of explosives.{{sfn|Morison|1953|pp=346–347}} 

[[File:USS Mazama (AE-9) c1956.jpg|thumb|right|Ammunition ship {{USS|Mazama|AE‑9|2}}]]
The risk involved in using untrained personnel to handle explosives was demonstrated by an incident at [[Naval Station Pearl Harbor|Pearl Harbor]]. Sixteen LSTs were designated to each carry 750 rounds of [[5-inch/38-caliber gun]] anti-aircraft ammunition and ten more were designated to each carry 270 [[M8 (rocket)|4.5-inch rockets]], 6,000 rounds of [[Bofors 40 mm L/60 gun|40 mm]] and 15,000 rounds of [[Oerlikon 20 mm cannon|20 mm]] anti-aircraft ammunition. It was intended to deploy three [[Landing Craft, Tank]], (LCTs) as gunships armed with six [[M2 4.2-inch mortar]] and 2,500 rounds, but two of them were lost when they slid off the decks of their LSTs in rough weather on the night of 14–15 May during a rehearsal of the landing operation. Turner decided to abandon the LCT gunship project and transfer the mortar ammunition to LSTs. On 21 May, as mortar rounds were being transferred to ''LST-353'' by the African-American 29th Chemical Decontamination Company, who had no training in handling ammunition, a mortar round exploded. In the resulting fires and secondary explosions, six LSTs and three LCTs were lost. The [[West Loch Disaster]] cost the lives of at least 163 men; another 396 were injured. Six LSTs originally earmarked to carry the garrison troops were substituted for the ones lost. Loading and embarkation was delayed by only one day. The LSTs departed Pearl Harbor on 25 May and made up the lost day en route.{{sfn|Dyer|1969|pp=893–895}}{{sfn|Crowl|1960|p=49}}<ref>{{cite news |title=Navy recalls bravery at WWII's West Loch Disaster|newspaper=[[The San Diego Union-Tribune]]|date=19 May 2009|url-access=subscription|url=https://www.sandiegouniontribune.com/2009/05/19/navy-recalls-bravery-at-wwiis-west-loch-disaster/|access-date=24 July 2025|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250724235034/https://www.sandiegouniontribune.com/2009/05/19/navy-recalls-bravery-at-wwiis-west-loch-disaster/|archive-date=24 July 2025|url-status=live}}</ref>

[[File:80-G-K-14228 USS New Mexico (BB-40) replenishes her ammunition supply prior to the invasion of Guam.tif|thumb|left|upright|{{USS|New Mexico|BB-40|6}} replenishes her ammunition supply prior to the invasion of Guam]]
The Battle of the Philippine Sea and staunch Japanese resistance ashore resulted in a longer campaign than anticipated, with increased calls for naval gunfire support, and consequently prodigious ammunition consumption.{{sfn|Morison|1953|pp=346–347}} Not long after the fighting began on Saipan, it became apparent the supply of certain types of ammunition, notably 6-inch high capacity (HC), [[5-inch/38-caliber gun#Ammunition|5-inch anti-aircraft common]] (AAC) and [[star shell]], would soon be exhausted.{{sfn|Carter|1953|p=151}} Out of a total of 106,110 shells ({{convert|8,429.6|ST|t}}) fired during Operation Stevedore, the liberation of Guam, 5,039 were star shells.{{sfn|Shaw|Nalty|Turnbladh|1994|p=573}} The higher than expected expenditure of ammunition ashore was exacerbated by the early commitment of the 27th Infantry Division to the fighting on Saipan, which meant three rather than two divisions were consuming ammunition at a high rate.{{sfn|Shaw|Nalty|Turnbladh|1994|pp=242–243}} The allocation of seven units of fire, which was based on previous campaigns in the Central Pacific, proved to be too low, and it was recommended ten be taken in future operations.{{sfn|Crowl|1960|pp=128–129}} This problem was not restricted to ammunition: signal units had brought twenty days' supply of batteries, but it was found this was not enough for twenty days' combat on Saipan.{{sfn|Shaw|Nalty|Turnbladh|1994|p=350}} 

Most resupply ships were not vertically loaded, which made retrieving the most urgently needed ammunition more problematic.{{sfn|Crowl|1960|pp=128–129}} As ''Mazama'' was loaded with the types most needed, she was called forward to replenish the fleet off Saipan while the battle still raged on shore, a risky action, as Japanese air attacks occurred almost every day.{{sfn|Carter|1953|p=151}}{{sfn|Morison|1953|pp=346–347}} Between 21 June and 7 July, when she returned to Eniwetok, ''Mazama'' discharged {{convert|3448|ST|t}} of ammunition.{{sfn|Carter|1953|p=152}}
===Aircraft===
[[File:80-G-238680.jpg|thumb|right|{{USS|Manila Bay|CVE-61|6}} under attack by four Japanese "Zeke" aircraft off Saipan on 23 June 1944. The deck is loaded with [[Republic P-47 Thunderbolt]] fighters for delivery to airfields on Saipan.]]
Replacement aircraft were brought out by four [[escort carrier]]s. Two, {{USS|Copahee|CVE-12|6}} and {{USS|Breton|CVE-23|2}}, carried naval aircraft, while {{USS|Manila Bay|CVE-61|6}} and {{USS|Natoma Bay|CVE-62|2}} brought twenty-two U.S. Army [[Republic P-47 Thunderbolt]] fighters of the [[19th Fighter Squadron]]. These were catapulted from the escort carriers on 22 June and landed on Isely Field on Saipan, where they were based for the rest of the campaign.{{sfn|Wildenberg|1996|pp=183–185}}{{sfn|Olson|Mortensen|1950|pp=690–691}} Although the escort carriers were designed to operate no more than twenty-four aircraft, various innovations allowed them to carry many more.{{sfn|Carter|1953|p=140}} 

For Operation Forager, ''Copahee'' took on 61 aircraft: 25 fighters, 15 torpedo bombers and 21 dive bombers. She left Pearl Harbor on 3 June with the oiler group. On 14 June, she delivered four fighters and a torpedo bomber to {{USS|Cowpens|CVL-25|6}}; a fighter, a torpedo bomber and three dive bombers to {{USS|Hornet|CV-12|6}}; four fighters to {{USS|Bataan|CVL-29|6}}; five fighters, five torpedo bombers and seven fighters to {{USS|Yorktown|CV-10|6}}; and four fighters and two torpedo bombers to {{USS|Belleau Wood|CVL-24|6}}. In exchange, ''Copahee'' received flyable duds, aircraft capable of taking off and landing, but for one reason or another not capable of combat operations. During Operation Forager, ''Copahee'' shuttled back and forth between the Marianas and Eniwetok. On 7 July, it took back captured Japanese aircraft.{{sfn|Fisher|2023|pp=153–155}}{{sfn|Carter|1953|p=145}} Spare parts for aircraft were carried aboard {{USS|Fortune|IX-146|6}}.{{sfn|Carter|1953|p=140}}

===Salvage===
[[File:USS Hector (AR-7) underway at sea on 17 April 1985 (6403814).jpg|thumb|left|Repair ship {{USS|Hector|AR-7|6}}]]
Six [[fleet tug]]s accompanied the expeditionary force for towing and fire fighting, along with two salvage vessels; two more were assigned to Service Squadron 12 for harbor clearance. Two repair ships for landing craft, {{USS|Egeria||6}} and {{USS|Agenor||2}}, accompanied the expeditionary force. Battle damage to warships was addressed by the repair ships of Service Squadron 10 at Eniwetok.{{sfn|Carter|1953|p=141}} 

Service Squadron 10 was commanded by Commodore [[Worrall Reed Carter]], who flew his pennant from the [[destroyer tender]] {{USS|Prairie|AD-15|6}}. The squadron's ships included the repair ships {{USS|Vestal|AR-4|6}}, {{USS|Ajax|AR-6|2}}, {{USS|Hector|AR-7|2}} and {{USS|Luzon|ARG-2|2}}; destroyer tenders {{USS|Piedmont||6}}, {{USS|Cascade||2}} and {{USS|Markab||2}}; landing craft repair ship ''Egeria''; floating dry docks ''ARD-13'', ''ARD-15'' and ''AFD-15''; and floating workshop ''YR-30''.{{sfn|Carter|1953|p=163}}{{sfn|Morison|1953|p=420}}

A major salvage effort began on 21 July when the SS ''Sea Flyer'' ran aground at Eniwetok. The ship's {{convert|1,900|LT|t}} cargo had to be unloaded before she could be refloated and tugboats could tow her clear on 28 July.{{sfn|Carter|1953|p=167}}

===Medical===
[[File:USS Relief (AH-1) in a western Pacific harbour c1945.jpg|thumb|right|Hospital ship {{USS|Relief|AH-1|6}}]]
Medical supplies were carried in general stores ships. Four [[hospital ship]]s participated in Operation Forager: {{USS|Relief|AH-1|6}}, {{USS|Solace|AH-5|2}}, {{USS|Bountiful||2}} and {{USS |Samaritan||2}}. The transports {{USS|Rixey||6}}, {{USS|Wharton||2}} and {{USS|Tryon||2}} took walking wounded who required no more than two weeks hospitalization.{{sfn|Carter|1953|p=141}}{{sfn|Shaw|Nalty|Turnbladh|1994|p=502}} Three LSTs were specially equipped as makeshift hospital ships and handled 1,549 casualties on 15 June. ''Bountiful'' and ''Solace'' arrived off Saipan on 18 June and embarked 1,099 casualties. By 20 June, ''Solace'' was full to capacity with 584 cases on board and sailed for hospitals on [[Guadalcanal]]. She return to Saipan on 3 July, and took on 562 patients by the afternoon of 5 July, when she sailed to the [[Russell Islands]] to transfer them to Fleet Hospital 110 and the Army's 222nd Station Hospital. She then sailed for Guam via Eniwetok, where she refueled, and arrived at Agana Bay on 24 July to take on a capacity of 585 cases, who were taken to Kwajalein. ''Relief'' and ''Samaritan'' took 1,355 casualties on board on 23 June. On her second voyage to Saipan, ''Relief'' embarked 685, of whom 284 were wounded Japanese.{{sfn|Carter|1953|pp=158–160}} Seaplanes from [[VH-1 (Rescue Squadron)|VH-1]] performed emergency evacuation of casualties to the Marshall Islands.{{sfn|Carter|1953|p=141}} Air evacuation from [[Isely Field]] commenced on 24 June, and 860 casualties were flown to the Marshall Islands during Operation Forager.{{sfn|Carter|1953|pp=158–160}}

==Ship-to-shore==
Each transport division consisted of four APAs and an attack cargo ship (AKA). Each transport carried up to 1,500 troops, so a transport division could carry a [[regimental combat team]]. Three transport divisions were required to lift an infantry or marine division, with another four APAs and four AKAs for corps troops.{{sfn|Morison|1953|pp=347–349}} The landings in the Marianas required [[Landing Vehicle, Tracked|landing vehicles, tracked]], (LVTs) to carry troops across the reefs.{{sfn|Crowl|1960|p=342}} Their value had been demonstrated in the [[Battle of Tarawa]].{{sfn|Shaw|Nalty|Turnbladh|1994|p=582}} They were carried in [[Landing Ships, Tank]] (LSTs). The LVTs were able to cross the reef and disembark their troops on the beach.{{sfn|Crowl|1960|p=342}} 

[[File:Blue beach 1 Saipan.jpg|thumb|right|Shore Party on Blue Beach 1, Saipan]]
Only the first waves of infantry were in LVTs; succeeding waves arrived in assault transports and were loaded onto [[LCVP (United States)|landing craft, vehicle, personnel]], (LCVPs) carried by the assault transports. The LCVPs carried the troops to the reef, where they transferred to LVTs returning after dropping off earlier waves.{{sfn|Crowl|1960|p=342}} Tanks were carried in LSDs. Each LSD carried twenty medium tanks, loaded in a LCT and fourteen [[Landing Craft, Mechanized]] (LCMs).{{sfn|Shaw|Nalty|Turnbladh|1994|pp=458–459}} Eight LSTs were required to carry the Marines' divisional artillery for the assault on Saipan.{{sfn|Morison|1953|pp=347–349}} The [[M101 howitzer|105 mm howitzers]] were carried in [[DUKW]]s (amphibious trucks) while LVTs brought the [[M116 howitzer|75 mm pack howitzer]]s from the reefs. Once the situation ashore stabilized, LSTs could nose up to the reef and discharge vehicles and supplies directly onto it.{{sfn|Shaw|Nalty|Turnbladh|1994|pp=458–459}} Six LSTs carried four pontoon barges each, six carried two pontoon causeway section, and thirty carried an LCT.{{sfn|Morison|1953|pp=347–349}}

[[File:USMC 87833 Guam Invasion, 1944. A DUKW drives down the ramp of USS LST-220, during re-supply operations on the Guam beachhead, July-August 1944.tif|thumb|left|A [[DUKW]] drives down the ramp of USS ''LST-220'' during re-supply operations on Guam]]
While the troops could move inland under their own power, supplies could not, and had to remain on the beach until the shore party could sort them and transport became available to move them to inland dumps. Supplies therefore piled up on the beaches. On Saipan, hydrographic conditions were not conducive to a steady flow of supplies over the beaches. The beaches were under mortar and artillery fire for the first four days, which created further disruption as unloading activity was suspended while the shore parties sought cover. The appearance of the Japanese fleet caused the transports to move out to sea on each of the first three nights, and they left the area completely on the next five days and nights while they awaited the outcome of the Battle of the Philippine Sea.{{sfn|Crowl|1960|pp=123–124}}

Naval beach parties marked the beaches and supervised the unloading of stores from the ships and their movement to the shoreline. These were under the control of the naval beachmasters. Each assault division had a transport group beachmaster. In turn, each transport group beachmaster had two transport division beachmasters, one for each assault regiment. Each division had a shore party working in cooperation with the beachmasters. Marine divisions were authorized to have a shore party of 98 officers and 2,781 enlisted; that of the 2nd Marine Division was built around its pioneer battalion, whereas that of the 4th Marine Division was built around the 121st Naval Construction Battalion. Each regiment of the 27th Infantry Division had an engineer battalion functioning as its shore party: the 34th, 152th and 1341st Engineer Combat battalions of the 1165th Engineer Combat Group.{{sfn|Crowl|1960|pp=123–124}}

[[File:80-G-47214 (20842096224).jpg|thumb|right|Floating refueling point off Tinian]]
On Saipan, supplies intended for the 2nd Marine Division were often dumped on the 4th Marine Division's beaches and vice versa, but the situation deteriorated further when the 27th Infantry Division was called forward from floating reserve. Plans had not been made for it to land in the 4th Marine Division area, and the beachmaster would not agree to land all of its supplies on a single beach. As a result, its supplies were landed on several beaches. When the transports retired on 18 June, the division was left with four days' supply of small arms ammunition and just 600 rounds of [[M114 155 mm howitzer|155 mm howitzer]] and 1,200 rounds of 105 mm ammunition per battalion. Only three of its [[2½-ton 6×6 truck]]s had landed, although it still had forty-nine DUKWs. The 27th Infantry Division's supplies were not fully unloaded until 27 June. The handling of its supplies was greatly eased, though, by the large proportion that arrived on pallets.{{sfn|Crowl|1960|pp=125–128}}

The 77th Infantry Division's experience with pallets was less successful because the beaches on Guam lay beyond a coral reef. Cranes were required to load the pallets, and these were not always available, so many pallet loads were broken up so supplies could be manhandled to transfer points. Landing craft and landing ships could not come closer than the reef, several hundred yards from the beach, and the loss of twenty-four LVTs to Japanese fire in the initial assault was sorely felt. A layer of silt on the shore side of the reef caused DUKWs and LVTs to become bogged, slowing things down. Rubber raft causeways and ships' life rafts were used as expedients to get the needed supplies ashore.{{sfn|Bruce|1944|p=6}}{{sfn|Morison|1953|pp=390–391}}

Cranes were used to shift supplies in [[cargo net]]s from landing craft to LVTs and DUKWs. Unlike the northern beaches on Guam, where some cranes were set up on the reef, all the cranes on the southern beaches had to be mounted on barges, with cargo transferred in deep water.{{sfn|Lodge|1954|p=54}}{{sfn|Shaw|Nalty|Turnbladh|1994|p=478}}{{sfn|Bruce|1944|p=7}} Although an additional 25 mobile cranes were provided to the Marines, there were still insufficient numbers for the multiple transfers required at the reef, beach and dumps.{{sfn|Shaw|Nalty|Turnbladh|1994|pp=577–578}} The Navy assisted the forces ashore by continuing unloading operations through the night under lights with the aid of the Army shore parties' 5 KW generators.{{sfn|Lodge|1954|p=54}}{{sfn|Shaw|Nalty|Turnbladh|1994|p=478}}{{sfn|Bruce|1944|p=7}} The Marine pioneer battalions were less well-equipped than their Army counterparts with lighting required for round-the-clock beach operations, and some units had antiquated equipment with batteries that failed, but the Army shore parties had sufficient lights to meet the requirements of all the beaches.{{sfn|Shaw|Nalty|Turnbladh|1994|pp=577–578}}

[[File:Pontoon causeway and barge in Charan Kanoa Harbor.jpg|thumb|left|Supplies are unloaded over a pontoon causeway and barge in Charan Kanoa Harbor]]
About one-fifth of the total strength of III Amphibious Corps was engaged in unloading activities. The 1st Provisional Marine Brigade initially had 1,070 troops on board ship and 1,800 on the reef and beaches engaged in unloading work. The 77th Infantry Division, with three battalions of shore party engineers and 270 garrison troops with low landing priorities, employed 583 soldiers unloading ships and 1,828 working ashore. The Marines used a unit called the 1st Provisional Replacement Company to provide shore party labor in the early stages of the landing on Guam when the demand for labor was at its highest. After the initial flood of supplies ashore, unloading work became more organized and routine, and the personnel of the 1st Provisional Replacement Company were fed into combat units to replace casualties. This concept proved sound, and provisional replacement battalions were employed in this manner in subsequent operations in the Pacific.{{sfn|Shaw|Nalty|Turnbladh|1994|pp=577–578}} Potential supply dump locations identified beforehand from aerial photos were often found to be rice paddies, which were unsuitable.{{sfn|Simmons|1987|p=8}}

To provide the needs of units ashore, the Marines formed two new units for Operation Forager, the 5th and 7th Field Depots. The former supported the III Amphibious Corps on Guam and the latter the V Amphibious Corps on Saipan and Tinian. They repaired weapons and equipment, carried out salvage, and sorted, stockpiled and issued stores and ammunition.{{sfn|Shaw|Nalty|Turnbladh|1994|pp=242–243}} As the ground units moved north on Guam, the impact of heavy rains and a shortage of bulldozers and graders to maintain the roads became evident, and there were insufficient trucks to maintain the target of five days' of supply in the forward dumps. The requirement to feed thousands of civilians had not been anticipated, and the reserves of rations were depleted below the desired ten days' of supply but did not run out, and the 5th Field Depot was able to maintain twenty days' supply of most items.{{sfn|Shaw|Nalty|Turnbladh|1994|pp=577–578}}

