==Base development==
===Saipan===
{{main|Naval Advance Base Saipan}}
The Seabees of the 18th and 121st Naval Construction Battalions with elements of 67th and 92nd Naval Construction Battalions landed on Saipan with the assault troops on 15 June 1944. [[Aslito Airfield]] was captured on 18 June. Holes were filled with crushed [[coral]] and the {{convert|4,500|ft|adj=on}} runway resurfaced with [[marston mat]]. By 21 June, it was widened to {{convert|200|ft}}. The Japanese gasoline storage blockhouses were still usable and were restocked by the Seabees. The Army's 804th Engineer Aviation Battalion then took over construction.{{sfn|Building the Navy's Bases|1947|p=340}} The Army's 19th Fighter Squadron arrived on 22 June, followed over the next two days by the [[73rd Fighter Squadron]] and a detachment of seven [[Northrop P-61 Black Widow]] night fighters from the [[6th Night Fighter Squadron]].{{sfn|Olson|Mortensen|1950|pp=690–691}} The airfield was renamed Isley Field in honor of Commander Robert H. Isely, a naval aviator killed in the preliminary air strikes on the island.{{sfn|Taylor|Cate|Olson|Futrell|1953|p=515}}{{sfn|Dod|1966|p=498}} 
[[File:Isley Field Saipan 1945.jpg|thumb|left|Isely Field in 1945]]
The Army's 1176th Construction Group, under the command of Colonel [[Brendan A. Burns]], who doubled as garrison engineer,{{sfn|Dod|1966|p=511}} undertook the construction of a new {{convert|8,500|ft|adj=on}} [[asphalt concrete|asphalt]]-surfaced runway at Isley Field for [[Boeing B-29 Superfortress]] bombers. The new runway ran across the existing one, so in order to not interrupt fighter operations, work proceeded on the two ends of the runway, leaving the existing airstrip untouched. The topsoil was not deep, and underneath was coral rock that had to be removed by blasting, for which the 804th Engineer Aviation Battalion used about five tons of dynamite each day. Asphalt was produced at plants located near the field, and coral rock came from a quarry {{convert|2|mi}} away. The engineers laid down a coral base {{convert|12|to|18|in|cm}} deep.{{sfn|Dod|1966|pp=498–501}}<ref>{{cite news |title=Our B-29 Base: An Epic Job; The story of how Saipan was remade to give a base for bombing Tokyo|newspaper=[[The New York Times]]|first=Clinton|last=Green|date=10 December 1944|page=8|url=https://www.nytimes.com/1944/12/10/archives/our-b29-base-an-epic-job-the-story-of-how-saipan-was-remade-to-give.html|access-date=19 July 2025}}</ref>

A second strip was begun to the northwest, but the difficulty encountered was so great it was only developed as a shorter, medium bomber strip. Another B-29 runway was constructed at Isley, parallel to the first.{{sfn|Dod|1966|p=511}} The first B-29 of the [[73rd Bombardment Wing]] landed on Saipan on 12 October, before work on the first runway was completed. The second was in use by 15 December, but works at Isley were not completed until April 1945.{{sfn|Taylor|Cate|Olson|Futrell|1953|p=517}} 

[[File:Railroad on Saipan.jpg|thumb|right|Charan Kanoa sugar mill and railroad tracks on Saipan, 16 June 1944]]
On 21 June, Seabees of the 121st Naval Construction Battalion began to repair the shell-damaged railroad tracks from [[Chalan Kanoa, Saipan|Charan Kanoa]] to Aslito Airfield, and by 25 June supply trains were running. Other tracks in the area were also repaired. A diesel and three steam locomotives were repaired and put to use, along with a hundred [[flatcar]]s. The railway, operated by the 1398th Engineer Construction Battalion, was carrying {{convert|350|ton-mile}} per day by July. The tracks were subsequently ripped up and a road built on the right of way. Two of the locomotives were used to sterilise garbage cans.{{sfn|Building the Navy's Bases|1947|p=340}}{{sfn|Dod|1966|pp=502–503}} After the capture of [[Tanapag Harbor]], the port was rehabilitated. The Army was responsible for building piers and cargo storage areas, while the Navy removed sunken vessels and other debris offshore and dredged the harbor.{{sfn|Dod|1966|p=501}} 

On 13 September 1944, the Seabees were given the order to turn Saipan into a major advance naval base. The 39th, 17th, 101st, 117th, 595th and 614th Construction Battalions and the 31st Special Battalion joined in the construction. To keep boats and LVTs on the fighting front, there was great demand for bases that could repair and restock boats in remote ports.{{sfn|Building the Navy's Bases|1947|p=342}} Hospitals were initially in tents, but over time these were replaced by [[Quonset hut]]s, and water, sewage, air-conditioning, asphalt roads and concrete sidewalks were added.{{sfn|Dod|1966|p=502}} The 17th Naval Construction Battalion built a 400-bed hospital in early 1945, which received casualties from the [[Battle of Iwo Jima]] and the [[Okinawa campaign]]. Army hospitals built on Saipan included the 3,000-bed 5th Convalescent Hospital and the 2,000-bed 148th General Hospital, both of which were completed in April 1945, the 39th General Hospital and the 176th and 369th Station Hospitals.{{sfn|Building the Navy's Bases|1947|p=343}}{{sfn|Condon-Rall|Cowdrey|1998|p=236}}

===Tinian===
{{main|Tinian Naval Base}}
Responsibility for the transformation of Tinian into a base for B-29 bombers was assigned to the 6th Naval Construction Brigade, commanded by Captain [[Paul J. Halloran]]. For this work his brigade had the 29th and 30th Naval Construction Regiments; a third regiment, the 49th Naval Construction Regiment, arrived in March 1945.<ref>{{cite web |title=6th Naval Construction Brigade Log: Task on Tinian |publisher=United States Navy Seabee Museum |pages=8–14 |url=https://www.history.navy.mil/content/dam/museums/Seabee/Cruisebooks/wwiicruisebooks/misc-cruisebooks/6%20%20Naval%20Construction%20Brigade.pdf |access-date=22 March 2024 |archive-date=20 February 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240220224827/https://www.history.navy.mil/content/dam/museums/Seabee/Cruisebooks/wwiicruisebooks/misc-cruisebooks/6%20%20Naval%20Construction%20Brigade.pdf |url-status=live }}</ref> Two air bases were constructed, [[North Field (Tinian)|North Field]] and [[West Field (Tinian)|West Field]]. These were on the site of the existing Japanese fields at Ushi Point and Gurguan Point respectively, but they had to be lengthened to {{convert|8500|ft}} and widened to {{convert|500|ft}} to handle the B-29s. This task would have been easier if the plateau had been more than {{convert|7000|ft}} wide. As it was, large amounts of fill were required.{{sfn|Building the Navy's Bases|1947|pp=358–362}}
[[File:Seabee directs truckers to unload coral for B-29 bomber runway on Tinian.jpg|thumb|left|Seabee Vance Shoemate directs truckers to unload coral for B-29 bomber runway on Tinian. In the background is a fleet of trucks awaiting their turn for leads from the scoop shovel.]]

When work was completed on 5 May 1945, North Field had four parallel {{convert|8500|ft|adj=on}} runways, with {{convert|8|mi}} of [[taxiway]]s, 265 [[hardstand]]s, 173 Quonset huts and 92 other buildings. Its construction involved {{convert|2109800|cuyd}} of excavations and {{convert|4789400|cuyd}} of fill. West Field had two B-29 runways, {{convert|53000|ft}} of taxiways, 220 hardstands and 251 administration, maintenance and repair buildings. The adjacent base for naval aircraft had {{convert|16000|ft}} of taxiways, 70 hardstands, 345 Quonset huts, 33 administration, maintenance and repair buildings, and a {{convert|75|ft|adj=on}} tall [[control tower]].{{sfn|Building the Navy's Bases|1947|pp=358–362}}

Fuel had to initially be supplied in drums. Later, [[aviation gasoline]] was drawn from a barge anchored in Tinian Harbor. The fuel storage and distribution system was completed by 8 March 1945. This included storage tanks for {{convert|14000|USbbl|l}} of [[diesel oil]], {{convert|20000|USbbl|l}} of [[motor gasoline]] and {{convert|165000|USbbl|l}} of aviation gasoline. Fuel was pumped over a submarine pipeline from an oil tanker moored north of Tinian Harbor and distributed over {{convert|86000|ft}} of pipeline.{{sfn|Building the Navy's Bases|1947|p=365}} Work on the harbor included dredging operations and the construction of a breakwater and quays for [[Liberty ship]]s. Until it was completed in March 1945, cargo was brought ashore by LCMs and LCTs.{{sfn|Building the Navy's Bases|1947|pp=366–367}}

North Field became operational in February 1945 and West Field the following month.{{sfn|Rottman|Gerrard|2004|p=89}} The [[313th Bombardment Wing]] arrived from the United States in December 1944 and was based at North Field. The [[58th Bombardment Wing]] arrived from the [[China-Burma-India]] Theater in March 1945 and was based at West Field. A third formation, the [[509th Composite Group]], arrived in May 1945 and moved to North Field, where it took over an area specially constructed for it. Thus, two of the five bombardment wings of the [[Twentieth Air Force]] were based on Tinian.{{sfn|Cate|1953|p=166}}{{sfn|Taylor|Cate|Olson|Futrell|1953|pp=519–525}} These formations participated in the campaign of [[air raids on Japan]], including the [[bombing of Tokyo (10 March 1945)|bombing of Tokyo on 10 March 1945]],{{sfn|Taylor|Cate|Olson|Futrell|1953|pp=614–617}} and the [[atomic bombings of Hiroshima and Nagasaki]] on 6 and 9 August 1945.{{sfn|Taylor|Cate|Olson|Futrell|1953|pp=713–725}}

The Navy disestablished the base on Tinian on 1 December 1946,{{sfn|Roberts|2000|p=767}} but the United States military remained on the island.<ref>{{cite web |title=Tinian Island |publisher=U.S. National Park Service |url=https://www.nps.gov/articles/000/tinian-island-during-the-manhattan-project.htm |access-date=11 December 2025}}</ref> In 2023, concerns about a conflict with China led to Tinian being reactivated as an alternative base,<ref>{{cite magazine |date=3 March 2023 |first=Chris |last=Gordon |title=Photos: F-22s Deploy to Tinian for First Time as Part of ACE Exercise  |magazine= Air & Space Forces Magazine |url=https://www.airandspaceforces.com/photos-f-22s-deploy-to-tinian-for-first-time-as-part-of-ace-exercise/ |access-date=13 April 2024}}</ref> and in 2024 [[Fluor Corporation]] was awarded a $409 million contract to rebuild the airbase at North Field.<ref>{{cite news |first=Noah |last=Robertson |date=11 April 2024 |title=US Air Force issues $409 million award for long-sought Pacific airfield |newspaper=Defense News |url=https://www.defensenews.com/global/asia-pacific/2024/04/10/us-air-force-issues-409-million-award-for-long-sought-pacific-airfield/ |access-date=13 April 2024}}</ref>

===Guam===
In the months after the battle, Guam was transformed into a major supply, naval and air base, and eventually became the location of Nimitz's headquarters.{{sfn|Shaw|Nalty|Turnbladh|1994|p=571}} For this purpose, the 5th Naval Construction Brigade was activated at Pearl Harbor on 1 June 1944 under the command of Captain William O. Hiltabidle Jr., with the 26th, 27th and 28th (Special) Naval Construction Regiments assigned. The first [[seabees in World War II|seabees]], the 25th and 53rd Construction Battalions, 2nd and 13th Special Battalions, and Construction Battalion Maintenance Unit 515, arrived with the assault troops, and mainly performed stevedoring duties. The 5th Naval Construction Brigade staff came ashore on 23 July.<ref name="5th Naval Construction Brigade">{{cite web |title=5th Naval Construction Brigade - Historical Information|publisher=Naval History and Heritage Command|pages=2–3|url=https://www.history.navy.mil/content/dam/museums/Seabee/UnitListPages/Brigades-WWII/05%20NCBr.pdf|access-date=6 July 2025|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250705204450/https://www.history.navy.mil/content/dam/museums/Seabee/UnitListPages/Brigades-WWII/05%20NCBr.pdf|archive-date=5 July 2025|url-status=live}}</ref>

[[File:Glass Breakwater under construction by Seabees of the 76th Construction Battalion at Apra Harbor, Guam - NARA 520745 (cropped).jpg|thumb|right|Seawall under construction by Seabees of the 76th Construction Battalion at Apra Harbor. A 30-ton boulder is hauled to a dumping spot.]]
The first order of business was the development of [[Apra Harbor]]. Work commenced on a pontoon pier on [[Cabras Island]] on 5 August. This was completed on 22 August, and by October six {{convert|42|by|350|ft|adj=on}} piers were in operation. Between 3 and 6 October, Guam was struck by a typhoon that wrecked all the piers and severely damaged the barges that had been sunk to create a [[breakwater (structure)|breakwater]]. The damage was repaired, and a new design was used to make the piers more resilient. [[Limestone]] was quarried on Cabras Island to construct a breakwater {{convert|3,260|ft}} in length and {{convert|32|ft}} in width. Development of the inner harbor involved {{convert|7,500,000|cuft}} of dredging.{{sfn|Building the Navy's Bases|1947|pp=349–350}}

The Japanese-built [[Orote Field]] was rebuilt and lengthened to {{convert|5,500|ft}} The airstrip near [[Hagåtña, Guam|Agana]] was extended to {{convert|7,000|ft}}, with the orientation shifted slightly to avoid Mount Barrigada, and a second runway was added. The first planes landed there on 22 October. A third airstrip, north of Agana Field, became Depot and later [[Harmon Air Force Base|Harmon Field]]. The {{convert|7,000|by|150|ft|adj=on}} was sealed with two inches of asphalt concrete and {{convert|12,000|ft}} of taxiway was constructed. The first B-29s landed there on 24 November.{{sfn|Building the Navy's Bases|1947|pp=350–352}} Two B-29 airfields, [[Andersen Air Force Base|North Field]] and [[Northwest Field]]s, were built by army aviation engineer battalions. North Field was commissioned on 3 February 1945,{{sfn|Building the Navy's Bases|1947|pp=350–352}}{{sfn|Brown|1945|pp=398–401}} and the first B-29 raid from Guam was launched from there on 24 February.{{sfn|Rottman|2004|p=88}} A second runway was operational by April. The south runway at Northwest Field became operational on 1 June, and the north runway on 1 July.{{sfn|Building the Navy's Bases|1947|pp=350–352}} 

The seabees and army engineers also built the Naval Base Hospital, Fleet Hospitals 103 and 115, and the Army's 373rd Station Hospital and 204th General Hospital. Their 9,000 beds were soon used for casualties from the Battle of Iwo Jima.{{sfn|Building the Navy's Bases|1947|p=357}} By August 1945, 201,718 American troops were based on Guam, of which 65,095 were Army and Army Air Forces, 77,911 were Navy, and 58,712 were Marines.{{sfn|Shaw|Nalty|Turnbladh|1994|p=571}}

Guam remained an important American base after the war. Naval Operating Base, Guam, was redesignated [[Naval Base Guam]], in 1952, and subsequently became a home port for submarines.{{sfn|Rottman|2002|p=393}} In 1947, the USAAF transferred Agana Airfield to the United States Navy, and it became [[Naval Air Station Agana]]. It was consolidated with neighboring [[Harmon Air Force Base]] in 1949, and operated until 1993, when it was closed by the [[Base Realignment and Closure]] (BRAC) Commission.<ref>{{cite web |title=Former Naval Air Station Agana|publisher=Base Realignment and Closure Commission|url=https://www.bracpmo.navy.mil/BRAC-Bases/Other-West/Former-Naval-Air-Station-Agana/|access-date=5 July 2025}}</ref> North Field became [[Andersen Air Force Base]] in 1947. During the [[Vietnam War]], Guam-based [[Boeing B-52 Stratofortress]] bombers flew missions over North Vietnam. Guam hosted 100,000 Vietnamese refugees in 1975, and 6,600 Kurdish refugees in 1996.{{sfn|Rottman|2002|p=393}} In 2009, U.S. Naval Base Guam and Andersen Air Force Base became part of [[Joint Region Marianas]],<ref>{{cite web |title=History|publisher=Joint Region Marianas|url=https://jrm.cnic.navy.mil/About/History/|access-date=5 July 2025|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250705040118/https://jrm.cnic.navy.mil/About/History/|archive-date=5 July 2025|url-status=live}}</ref> and in 2020, they were joined by a third newly created installation, [[Marine Corps Base Camp Blaz]].<ref>{{cite news |title='Solid progress': A look inside Camp Blaz, the Marine Corps' newest base on Guam|newspaper=Stars and Stripes|date=10 December 2022|first=Alex|last=Wilson|url=https://www.stripes.com/theaters/asia_pacific/2022-12-09/camp-blaz-guam-marine-corps-base-8365774.html|access-date=5 July 2025|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250705040117/https://www.stripes.com/theaters/asia_pacific/2022-12-09/camp-blaz-guam-marine-corps-base-8365774.html|archive-date=5 July 2025|url-status=live}}</ref>
